WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.052 --> 00:00:01.560
(cheerful music)

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.540
- [Narrator] This is
just a typical morning.

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:05.910
I'm eating some cereal
with lactose-free milk

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:07.710
as I watched some videos on the internet

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:09.060
using closed captioning,

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:12.450
while looking up directions
to my MRI appointment.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.430
Okay, maybe not really typical

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:16.050
but just about everything here,

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:18.960
the milk, the TV, the
graphics cards, the internet,

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.930
the closed captioning,
the GPS, the MRI machine

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:25.140
were made possible because
of government investments.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.360
- It's probably the case that
every technology used today,

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.577
at some point there was like
government funding involved.

00:00:29.577 --> 00:00:31.200
- [Narrator] And the latest technology

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.150
that the US government
is getting involved in

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.580
is manufacturing microchips.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:39.060
- Folks, we need to make
these chips here in America.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:39.893
- It's law.

00:00:39.893 --> 00:00:41.820
- President Biden just
signed a bill into law today

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:44.340
boosting domestic
semiconductor production.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:45.480
- There are some big companies

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:46.530
that are set to benefit.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.630
- If it's a good idea to
make the semiconductors here,

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:50.550
why do we need the government to help us?

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:51.780
- [Narrator] Here's why the US government

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:54.270
is giving private companies
billions of dollars

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:57.150
and why many economists
are calling the Chips Act

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.583
a return to industrial policy.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.360
- Industrial policy is
actually quite a simple term.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.220
It's a set of policies that
try to affect the outcome

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.163
of some particular industry or industries.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:13.200
- [Narrator] Industrial policy

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:14.970
can be done through things like tariffs

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:17.550
that make US made products
cheaper than foreign ones,

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:20.130
or through tax incentives
like getting money back

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:22.110
if you put solar panels on your house,

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.840
or what has been less
popular in recent years,

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.790
direct investments.

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:30.240
Let's go back, way back
to Alexander Hamilton.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.990
In 1790, Hamilton proposed
tariffs on imported goods

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:35.940
in order to encourage
his fellow countrymen

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.100
to buy more American products.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.440
- [Speaker] This is the
immense plant which gave birth

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.500
to the atomic bomb and brought
the world to a new crossroad.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.510
- [Narrator] Fast forward
to the 20th Century,

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:48.270
to prevent the spread of
radioactive contaminants

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.280
used during the Manhattan Project,

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:53.580
the US government funded the
development of the HEPA filter

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:57.360
which is used in many
air purifiers sold today.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.450
World War II also required
enormous investment

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:04.860
in radio technology, ship
building and jet engines.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:06.960
- The defense department was largely

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.600
the main buyer of many
of these technologies.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.490
The commercial market was quite small,

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:13.320
the defense market was quite big.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.090
And so they could really drive

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.460
a lot of the movement in industry.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:18.870
- [Narrator] In the 1960s,

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:20.400
the US government spent more money

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.890
on research and development
than all other governments,

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:24.390
all foreign private companies

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:27.060
and all US private companies combined.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.650
Why?

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.740
The Cold War and the Space Race?

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:33.120
- What did the space program give us?

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:34.560
It gave us Tang

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.110
- [Speaker] From Gemini to
the Shuttle to Earth families,

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:37.980
- [Both] Bye, mom.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:40.380
- [Speaker] Great mornings
have taken off with Tang.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:41.490
- Yeah, it gave us Tang,

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:44.430
but it also gave a gazillion other things.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.830
It wasn't talked about
as industrial policy.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:48.690
It was talked about as space policy

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:50.970
or defense policy or health policy,

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:53.460
but it was a defacto industrial policy.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:54.720
- [Speaker] And liftoff.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:56.580
- [Narrator] A defacto industrial policy

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:58.830
with many unforeseen benefits.

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:00.570
This robotic prosthetic leg

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:02.640
is powered by small, powerful motors,

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:05.100
originally designed for a robotic arm

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:06.873
on the International Space Station.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:11.970
Like many of these technologies,
semiconductors originated

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:13.740
from research and development programs

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:15.660
funded by the US taxpayer.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:17.460
- The birth of semiconductors
in the United States

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.770
had a lot to do with the fact
that the Department of Defense

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:21.300
and the space program were the first

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:24.450
big early customers for semiconductors.

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:27.450
- [Narrator] Since 2006,
China has prioritized

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:29.640
catching up to the west technologically.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.190
But doing that requires semiconductors.

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:34.620
- China has ramped up
its investment in Chips.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:36.750
- [Narrator] Senator Mark
Warner, who is the chairman

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:38.700
of the Senate Select
Committee on Intelligence

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:39.533
has long been warning

00:03:39.533 --> 00:03:41.670
about the growing threat posed by China.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:44.400
- China has been able to
throw literally, you know,

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.610
two, three, four times as much
money at trying to build up

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:51.300
their semiconductor chip
capacity as the United States.

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:53.250
- [Narrator] This is where
the Chips Act comes in.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:57.090
The legislation offers $53
billion in direct investment

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:59.310
plus a separate tax incentive program

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:01.650
to reinvigorate the US chip industry.

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:03.930
- So, what the Chips bill did was say,

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:05.670
we're gonna take 52 billion,

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:07.860
but we're going to take
about 40 billion of that

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:10.830
and try to get the semiconductor factories

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:12.300
that are called Fabs,

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:15.180
get the Fabs to locate here in America

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:17.130
- [Narrator] Today, the US still dominates

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:20.280
the most lucrative links in
the semiconductor supply chain

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:21.990
including the design of chips.

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:23.640
But the fabrication of chips

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:27.690
has increasingly moved to
China, South Korea, and Taiwan.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:29.490
- Over the last 20 years,

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:31.710
a lot of that industry
has migrated to East Asia

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:33.360
where land and labor are cheaper

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:35.250
and there are a lot of subsidies available

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:36.150
- [Narrator] Because of this,

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:37.920
the US share of global chip making

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:42.390
has shrunk from 37% in
1990 to 12% in 2020.

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:46.770
China went from zero to
about 15% in the same time.

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:50.370
- If we don't do this, I don't
think there'll be another

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:53.310
chip manufacturing
facility built in America.

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:56.580
- Senator Warner sees this
as a national security issue.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:59.490
- Over the last
particularly 15 or 20 years,

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:02.100
what we've seen is that national security

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:04.890
is no longer who has the
most military hardware

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:08.490
but also who controls
what technology domains.

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:10.650
- [Narrator] The US now buys more than 90%

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.690
of its advanced chips from Taiwan,

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:15.270
an island China sees as its territory.

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:16.680
Any conflict in Taiwan

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:19.530
could dramatically disrupt
global chip supplies.

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:21.750
- We all saw what happened
during the pandemic

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:24.180
when chip factories shut down overseas.

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:26.310
We can never let that happen again.

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:27.990
- We have never before been

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:31.560
in this kind of a Cold War type conflict

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:35.280
with a power that was also
a worthy economic peer.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:37.800
And I think it is awakened,
in the United States,

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:40.830
an awareness that we cannot simply allow

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:43.470
the private market to
allow vital technologies

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:46.410
and capabilities to entirely
migrate to a country

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:49.680
that we are in an
adversarial situation with.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:51.180
- So our competitors are pouring

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:54.570
tens of billion dollars into
boosting their own supply

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:56.940
of these essential semiconductors,

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:00.333
and the United States needs
to keep up and to compete.

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:03.040
The vast majority of our colleagues

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:07.320
agree that this was an
important and critical task.

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:08.153
- [Narrator] When was passed,

00:06:08.153 --> 00:06:11.170
the Chips Act received
rare bipartisan support.

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:14.610
The yays are 64, the nays are 33.

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:16.920
- There is broad bipartisan agreement

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:19.470
that China is the challenge of our time,

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:22.830
and that in these areas of technology

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:26.370
we can't allow America to fall behind.

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:28.020
- [Narrator] Critics of industrial policy

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.910
accuse it of picking winners

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:31.830
rather than letting the
free market determine

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:33.180
which companies succeed.

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:35.940
Some of the most famous
industrial policy failures

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:39.240
include investment targeted
at a single company.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.430
At former President Donald Trump's urging,

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:45.690
in 2017, Taiwan's Foxconn
Technology Group promised to build

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:48.240
a flat panel display factory in Wisconsin.

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:50.880
- This is a great day for American workers

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:53.160
and for everyone who believes

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:58.140
in the concept and the
label, Made in the USA.

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:01.650
- But five years later, and
that factory remains unbuilt.

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:04.860
Critics of industrial policy
also point to Solyndra

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:06.150
- When it's completed in a few months,

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:08.880
Solyndra expects to hire 1,000 workers

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:10.800
to manufacture solar panels

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:13.205
and sell them across America
and around the world.

00:07:13.205 --> 00:07:14.580
(audience applauding)

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:15.810
- [Narrator] But in 2011,

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:17.910
the solar manufacturer went bankrupt

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:22.910
after it defaulted on $535
million in federal loans.

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:25.350
But proponents of the Chips Act say,

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:27.360
it's not about picking specific firms

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:29.850
like Intel or Taiwan Semiconductor

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:33.360
- Good industrial policy
funds a variety of firms,

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:35.490
big, small, even foreign,

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:37.230
long as they're doing the work in the US

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:38.883
and they're not our adversaries.

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:42.780
- [Narrator] The ability
to apply for this money

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:45.690
is drawing major investment
from these firms right now.

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:48.750
Both Intel and TSMC are spending billions

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:50.610
to build new Fabs in the US.

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:51.720
- I think one thing we're gonna learn

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:54.150
is that industrial policy is expensive

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:56.430
and we have large government deficits.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:59.490
And so, I think one of the
things that will put the breaks

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:01.980
on the Rush to industrial
policy is the fact that

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:03.900
you'll have to persuade, you know,

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:06.630
Congress to vote for this money
and that's not easy to do.

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:08.400
The Republican senators, for example,

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:10.560
who gave their support to the Chips Act,

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:12.210
they did so because they saw this

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:14.670
as a narrow exception to
their general mistrust

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:16.650
of the government being
able to spend money

00:08:16.650 --> 00:08:18.000
better than the free market.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:19.260
And I think that there will not be

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:21.588
many other industries that meet that test.

00:08:21.588 --> 00:08:24.338
(cheerful music)

